<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Commands on STEP Batch 12</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/tags/commands/</link><description>Recent content in Commands on STEP Batch 12</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en_US</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 11 Sep 2026 14:30:27 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://step-12.github.io/tags/commands/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Commands</title><link>https://step-12.github.io/posts/commands/</link><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://step-12.github.io/posts/commands/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="commands"&gt;Commands&lt;a class="anchor" href="#commands"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ve seen that the shell reads what we type and either handles it itself or starts another process.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;But what exactly are we typing?&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;ls -l ~/Downloads&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a &lt;strong&gt;command&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s take it apart.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h3 id="the-anatomy-of-a-command"&gt;The Anatomy of a Command&lt;a class="anchor" href="#the-anatomy-of-a-command"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A command generally starts with a &lt;strong&gt;command name&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-text" data-lang="text"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;ls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Here, &lt;code&gt;ls&lt;/code&gt; is the name of the program we want to run.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;We can provide additional information to the command using &lt;strong&gt;arguments&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;options&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>